  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/59/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_52.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Giant-Man is shown catching a gang of thugs (apparently led by a guy named Nathan Garrett, who is a \"famous research scientist\").&nbsp; They were caught trying to sell \"scientific secrets to Red Chinese spies!\"&nbsp; Bail is set for Garrett at one hundred thousand dollars (a whopping sum back in 1963), but he knows the Reds will pay it rather than risk him talking.&nbsp; Sure enough, they do, and he catches a plane out to a small country in Europe -- a \"remote Balkan kingdom, nestled in the Alps.\"\n\nA few days later, he stares at a statue of a winged horse, fascinated by the legends of flying horses, and decides to put his scientific brain to the task of finding out if such creatures were ever possible.&nbsp; He rents a \"deserted castle\" and puts his mind to use.&nbsp; \"The days turn to weeks, to months\" until Garrett finally crosses eagle and stallion DNA to succeed!\n\n\"Weeks later\" after even that, back home in the States, Jan arrives (late) in Hank's lab and says she encountered a \"knight in black armor, riding a <i>mighty, flying horse</i>!\"&nbsp; While she watched, the Knight robbed an armored car and flew off!\n\nHank doesn't believe her, thinking she's just made up an excuse for being late, until they turn on the news (to which Jan says, \"It will be a <i>pleasure</i> to hear something besides the rattling of your test tubes!\").&nbsp; Sure enough, the coverage of the robbery is on the news.&nbsp; (\"I'm ready to accept your <i>apology</i> now, my dear Mr. Know-It-All!\")\n\nHank consults with his ant-watch guards, and they find the Black Knight going after a helicopter.&nbsp; Giant-Man catches a lift on an airplane and jumps down and catches hold of the helicopter.&nbsp; Before he jumps, he says to the guy at the open hatch that if he misses the copter, \"Tell the Wasp that -- aw forget it!&nbsp; She must <i>know</i> how I really feel about her!\"\n\nDangling from the bottom of the copter, he's finally in a position to challenge the Black Knight, and when the latter speaks, Giant-Man thinks he recognizes the voice.&nbsp; Wrapped up in a tangle of bolo balls, Hank appeals to his vanity to goad him into monologuing and realizes now this is Professor Garrett, who proceeds to brag about all the weapons he concealed in his lance while working in the castle's dungeon back in Europe.&nbsp; Finally Giant-Man drops from the helicopter and uses his free hand to take a reducing capsule, which allows him to get free of the bolo cables.\n\nWasp flies over to grab the now-Ant-Man-sized Hank and help lower him onto the flying horse unnoticed.&nbsp; Hank returns to normal size, and then pokes the lance out of the Black Knight's hand.&nbsp; The horse can't support both their weights and begins to lower toward an amusement park below, where Hank jumps off, leaving the Wasp to pinch the Black Knight's horse, who throws his rider off, where he lands below, inside a roller coaster car, which leads him straight toward the waiting Giant-Man below!\n\nBut the Wasp tries to control the horse by becoming normal-sized to no avail, and it throws <i>her</i> as well.&nbsp; Hank is forced to leave the Black Knight to rescue her.&nbsp; By the time they get back to him, the horse has returned to its master, and the Black Knight flies off with a cry of, \"You've beaten me <i>this</i> time, Giant-Man -- but I'll be back!&nbsp; Sooner or later  -- <i>I'll be back</i>!\"\n\nJan wonders why all Hank's enemies make such cornball exit speeches.",
  "grade_one_liner": "An evil Black Knight arrives and promises future importance.&nbsp; But otherwise,\nsurprisingly little happens, despite burning through months of story time.",
  "subtitle": "The Black Knight Arrives;&nbsp; The Plot Treads Water",
  "review_text": "The story's beginning has Giant-Man catching Nathan Garrett and a couple of others for the crime of \"delivering scientific secrets to Red Chinese spies,\" which only goes to show the rut that writer Stan Lee's brain must have been stuck in, because [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=just last issue], I talked about this <i>exact trope</i> (recall [LINK:1963:TTA_51_giant0|TTA #51|2027-02-09]'s \"Red Agent\" that the Human Top set out to meet).\n[ASSET: center | GarretBailout | width=67%]\n\n[ASSET: left | AnotherDesertedCastle | width=15%] But Stan wasn't done borrowing from previous stories.\n[BR] This one features... [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=wait for it]<i>..</i>. a \"deserted castle!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Really?]\n[BR] I literally called this out as a trope in <b>yesterday's review</b> ([LINK:1963:JIM_100_thor0|JIM #100|2027-03-04], published [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=the same day])!\n[BR]\n[BR] I'll <i>grant</i> this is Europe, where there are infinitely more castles than near NYC, but [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=still!]&nbsp; I can only guess that Stan Lee harbored a secret dream of someday inheriting an abandoned castle.\n\nThe origin of the Black Knight is gradually rolled out, beginning on pages 3 and 4, then continued during the \"monologue\" scene on page 10.&nbsp; From a storytelling perspective, it was a decent way to get back to the title heroes without bogging down the story, so that was a [SPAN: severity=success; text=good decision] on the part of Stan and his artist.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut tropes aside, the timeline in this issue feels [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>very</i> stretched out.]&nbsp; The time between Giant-Man's initial capture of the gang of thugs is \"months\" before Nathan Garrett becomes the Black Knight, and then more \"weeks\" before his debut in NYC.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=That's being pretty liberal with the passage of time!]\n\nAs Hank only became Giant-Man <i>recently</i>, burning through so many months in a single issue feels oddly wasteful.&nbsp; I'll keep an eye on this phenomenon as I proceed onward...\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | TrueFeelings | width=20%] Page 7 features Giant-Man jumping out of a plane [SPAN: severity=error; text=without a parachute.]&nbsp; Not only that, he jumps <i>down</i> onto a helicopter with [SPAN: severity=error; text=two spinning propeller blades!]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Talk about <i>reckless</i>!]\n\nI don't envision Hank routinely <i>practicing</i> such maneuvers, and even <i>he</i> seems to understand the risk he's taking when he starts to instruct the plane pilot to relay his true feelings for Jan... and then changes his mind.\n\n[ASSET: left | BlackKnightRecognized | width=12%] While Giant-Man battles the Black Knight (eventually recognizing him), he falls prey to some of the Iron Man-like gadgetry the villain has on hand, forcing him to plummet out of the sky.\n[BR]\n[BR] Jan later rescues him, but otherwise, her contributions are surprisingly <i>minimal</i> in this story, and she even ends up having to be rescued herself.&nbsp; Considering how effectively she's distracted opponents in prior adventures, I'd have expected her to spend at least <i>part</i> of the battle buzzing around the Black Knight's head.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn the end, Giant-Man has to divert his attention to saving the Wasp (who is thrown <i>off </i>said horse), but the delay allows the Black Knight to escape, complete with the immortal promise of [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"I'll be back!!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Decades</i> before Arnold Schwarzenegger made the phrase legendary!)]\n[ASSET: center | ExitSpeech | width=67%]\n\nJan calls out the \"corny exit\" speech, allowing Stan Lee to <i>wink</i> at the audience in acknowledgment that the adventure ultimately ends [SPAN: severity=info; text=with no resolution....]\n\nAs for Hank's \"honeychile\" term of endearment on the final panel, it's possible Stan was thinking of the 1951 comedy film of the same name, something a culturally-conscious writer such as he would likely have been familiar with.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nDick Ayers does the art for this story, and I have to admit he turned in a solid effort.&nbsp; I know I've commented before that he should stick to inking, but I'm big enough to give credit where it's due.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Good work on this issue!]\n\nHis choice of panel sizes was varied enough to keep the flow visually interesting (in particular his choice of much larger panels on pages 9 and 11).&nbsp; And he even remembers to omit the wings on the Wasp when she's full-sized, something Kirby doesn't seem to keep track of!\n\nThe Special Announcements Section of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16] points out that he did a bang-up job, [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=and I wholeheartedly concur.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nFrom the 50,000-foot view, the issue barely moves the needle forward.\n\nOther than the introduction of the Black Knight, there wasn't much here that felt particularly important, even though the story flowed okay, and the star characters acted in the manner we've come to expect of them.\n\nJan's near-constant flirting with Hank is omnipresent (even if her contributions were largely absent), and Hank continues to be portrayed as the all-too-serious scientist-turned-superhero that we've gradually gotten to know [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(and maybe even <i>mock</i> from time to time)].\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=Even so, for <i>Tales to Astonish</i>, this wasn't a bad issue at all, which is <i>saying</i> something, as this book has been dreadful of late.]\n\nThat's not to say that there weren't [SPAN: severity=error; text=negatives], namely:\n[HANG] * more secret plans being sold to Communist spies\n[HANG] * another deserted castle\n[HANG] * an extraordinarily stretched timeline\n[HANG] * reckless behavior of jumping <i>down</i> onto a helicopter (with <i>two blades</i>)\n[HANG] * Jan's near-vanishing act\n[HANG] * the villain's corny exit speech\n[HANG] * no real resolution, making the whole exercise feel empty\n\nAs I said above, the flow of the story wasn't the problem -- [SPAN: severity=success; text=that was solid enough, in fact.]\n\nUltimately, it's the introduction of the Black Knight that carries this issue's final grade.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Without that development, there simply isn't much here to remember.]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale</b>:&nbsp; \"Not What They Seem!\"\n\nIn the back-up tale, the Wasp once again shows up at an orphanage to amuse the kids with a story, this time about escaped convicts in the year 3000, who head out into space to find a place to hide, and of course they meet up with a Twilight Zone-like twist ending!&nbsp; This is <i>Tales to Astonish</i> after all!&nbsp; The word \"mannequin\" is again spelled \"manikin,\" which continues to look odd, though not actually incorrect."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "For the record, this is not the same Black Knight as Dane Whitman, who comes later, but <i>this</i> Black Knight does make other appearances in the near future, which we'll get to soon enough!",
  "highlight": "Giant-Man, the Wasp, and the newly minted Black Knight spend months getting to a story that barely moves the needle."
}